                <mxCell id="KvQJ0M6JbZ27sHgVb2HI-13" parent="KvQJ0M6JbZ27sHgVb2HI-10" style="text;html=1;whiteSpace=wrap;overflow=hidden;align=left;verticalAlign=top;spacing=0;fontFamily=Arial;fontSize=20;fontColor=#203746;fontStyle=1;rounded=1;" value="&lt;div style=&quot;text-align: center;&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: transparent;&quot;&gt;WO 12/7088 -&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: transparent; color: rgb(63, 63, 63);&quot;&gt;4Q 1813&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;" vertex="1">
                    <mxGeometry height="30" width="210.84" x="20" as="geometry"/>
                </mxCell>
                <mxCell id="KvQJ0M6JbZ27sHgVb2HI-14" parent="KvQJ0M6JbZ27sHgVb2HI-10" style="text;html=1;whiteSpace=wrap;overflow=hidden;align=center;verticalAlign=top;spacing=0;fontFamily=Arial;fontSize=20;fontColor=#455F70;fontStyle=0;rounded=1;" value="Voucher 1" vertex="1">
                    <mxGeometry height="30" width="225.45" x="5.389999999999873" y="30" as="geometry"/>
                </mxCell>
                <mxCell id="KvQJ0M6JbZ27sHgVb2HI-21" edge="1" parent="1" source="pZnFWdGS5KjD-QlC_WeW-12" style="edgeStyle=none;html=1;entryX=0.75;entryY=1;entryDx=0;entryDy=0;rounded=0;" target="KvQJ0M6JbZ27sHgVb2HI-20">
                    <mxGeometry relative="1" as="geometry"/>
                </mxCell>
                <mxCell id="KvQJ0M6JbZ27sHgVb2HI-24" edge="1" parent="1" source="pZnFWdGS5KjD-QlC_WeW-12" style="edgeStyle=none;html=1;entryX=0.5;entryY=1;entryDx=0;entryDy=0;rounded=0;" target="KvQJ0M6JbZ27sHgVb2HI-19">
                    <mxGeometry relative="1" as="geometry"/>
                </mxCell>
                <mxCell id="KvQJ0M6JbZ27sHgVb2HI-19" parent="1" style="verticalLabelPosition=middle;verticalAlign=middle;html=1;shape=trapezoid;perimeter=trapezoidPerimeter;whiteSpace=wrap;size=0.23;arcSize=10;flipV=1;labelPosition=center;align=center;rounded=0;" value="9 DAYS&lt;div&gt;AT SEA&lt;/div&gt;" vertex="1">
                    <mxGeometry height="60" width="100" x="4540" y="-1428" as="geometry"/>
                </mxCell>
                <mxCell id="KvQJ0M6JbZ27sHgVb2HI-20" parent="1" style="verticalLabelPosition=middle;verticalAlign=middle;html=1;shape=trapezoid;perimeter=trapezoidPerimeter;whiteSpace=wrap;size=0.23;arcSize=10;flipV=1;labelPosition=center;align=center;rounded=0;" value="14 DAYS&lt;div&gt;AT SEA&lt;/div&gt;" vertex="1">
                    <mxGeometry height="60" width="100" x="3440" y="-1438" as="geometry"/>
                </mxCell>
                <mxCell id="KvQJ0M6JbZ27sHgVb2HI-25" parent="1" style="shape=image;verticalLabelPosition=bottom;labelBackgroundColor=default;verticalAlign=top;aspect=fixed;imageAspect=0;image=data:image/jpeg,;rounded=0;" value="" vertex="1">
                    <mxGeometry height="316" width="200" x="4174" y="-1940" as="geometry"/>
                </mxCell>
                <mxCell id="P1l38svnj8LMgD6Zg53g-2" parent="1" style="shape=document;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;boundedLbl=1;size=0.11904761904761904;rounded=0;" value="&lt;div&gt;&lt;font color=&quot;#000000&quot;&gt;I followed him into the next set of paylists in WO12/7089, but abandoned photographing every entry, until I got to the quarter when the 7th btn was disbanded (Mar - Jun 1817).&amp;nbsp; Here Leven is annotated with a check mark with 2 lines through it; Notes on the paylist say that &#39;men checked out thus ..are charged sub[sequentl]y in 3d B[atallio]n 60th per Return voucher 6 of P[ay] L[ist] to Sep 17 of that Bn&#39;.&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;font color=&quot;#000000&quot;&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;font color=&quot;#000000&quot;&gt;The Paylists for the 3rd Batallion are filed after this, but I could not see Leven listed there.&amp;nbsp;&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/div&gt;" vertex="1">
                    <mxGeometry height="190" width="320" x="5790" y="-980" as="geometry"/>
                </mxCell>
                <mxCell id="P1l38svnj8LMgD6Zg53g-4" parent="1" style="shape=document;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;boundedLbl=1;size=0.11904761904761904;rounded=0;" value="&lt;div&gt;&lt;font color=&quot;#000000&quot;&gt;British TNA : Paylists for the 7th battalion of the 60th Foot, Levi&#39;s listed, and was there from 16 Dec 1813 until the 7th btn disbanded in 1817&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/div&gt;" vertex="1">
                    <mxGeometry height="70" width="340" x="5399.83" y="-950" as="geometry"/>
                </mxCell>
                <mxCell id="P1l38svnj8LMgD6Zg53g-5" parent="1" style="shape=document;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;boundedLbl=1;size=0.11904761904761904;rounded=0;" value="&lt;div&gt;&lt;font color=&quot;#000000&quot;&gt;SETTLEMENT&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;font color=&quot;#000000&quot;&gt;Regarding the circumstances of land grant, I located the following document (VF v. 139, #26):&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;font color=&quot;#000000&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;white-space: normal;&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;white-space:pre&quot;&gt;&#x9;&lt;/span&gt;&quot;Military Settlements in Nova Scotia After the War of 1812&quot;, J.S. Martell, &quot;read before the Nova Scotia&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;font color=&quot;#000000&quot;&gt;Historical Society, January 8, 1937&quot; [in &quot;Collections of the Nova Scotia Historical Society&quot;. p. 75-105]&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;font color=&quot;#000000&quot;&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;font color=&quot;#000000&quot;&gt;A portion of the text reads:&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;font color=&quot;#000000&quot;&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;font color=&quot;#000000&quot;&gt;(p. 93)&quot;In the meantime, about fifty Germans of the 7th battalion of the 60th had expressed their desire to settle&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;font color=&quot;#000000&quot;&gt;in Lunenberg county.&amp;nbsp; these men had fought with Wellington in Europe and with Sherbrooke at Castine, and&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;font color=&quot;#000000&quot;&gt;Dalhousie decided that he had no hesitation in suggesting that they receive a share of His Majesty&#39;s bounty.&quot;&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;font color=&quot;#000000&quot;&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;font color=&quot;#000000&quot;&gt;(p. 102)&quot;The Germans of the 60th, or those who remained, were not ready until the second grant was made up in 1821.&amp;nbsp; The grant of six thousand nine hundred acres, which went into effect in January, 1822, happily names the corps to which each man formerly belonged.&amp;nbsp; Of the forty-five grantees, twenty-four had been in the Nova Scotia Fencibles (two of them had appeared on the previous grant), four in the Royal Artillery, two in the Newfoundland Fencibles, and one in the 76th.&amp;nbsp; There were also two civilians, one the son of Captain Ross.&amp;nbsp; From the foregoing, it would appear that 109 soldiers were confirmed in the possession of the nineteen thousand nine hundred acres in Sherbrooke.&quot;&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/div&gt;" vertex="1">
                    <mxGeometry height="510" width="360" x="6180" y="-990" as="geometry"/>
                </mxCell>
                <mxCell id="P1l38svnj8LMgD6Zg53g-6" parent="1" style="shape=document;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;boundedLbl=1;size=0.11904761904761904;rounded=0;" value="&lt;div&gt;&lt;font color=&quot;#000000&quot;&gt;Research conducted in Nova Scotia Archives in 2000 by Allen Robertson commissoned by Todd DeMelle&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;font color=&quot;#000000&quot;&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;font color=&quot;#000000&quot;&gt;RG 20 &quot;A&quot;: Post-1800 [Land Grant Papers]: DeMill, Levi ---see: Ross, William (Lieut.) &amp;amp; Others 1822&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;font color=&quot;#000000&quot;&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;font color=&quot;#000000&quot;&gt;---Ross, William (Lieut.) &amp;amp; Others 1822: .... Draft Grant: to military settlers at Sherbrooke Settlement, 6900 acres of land in Lunenberg and Kings Counties.&amp;nbsp; [Includes several German names: ... eg. Kiens, Graff, Clotiz, Shipain, Hession]: .... Levi DeMill-- 200 acres.&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;font color=&quot;#000000&quot;&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;font color=&quot;#000000&quot;&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;font color=&quot;#000000&quot;&gt;***The Men checked out thus (*) are charged&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;font color=&quot;#000000&quot;&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;font color=&quot;#000000&quot;&gt;&#39;men checked out thus ..are charged sub[sequentl]y in 3d B[atallio]n 60th per Return voucher 6 of P[ay] L[ist] to Sep 17 of that Bn&#39;&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/div&gt;" vertex="1">
                    <mxGeometry height="340" width="390" x="6570" y="-990" as="geometry"/>
                </mxCell>
                <mxCell id="P1l38svnj8LMgD6Zg53g-7" parent="1" style="shape=document;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;boundedLbl=1;size=0.11904761904761904;rounded=0;" value="&lt;div&gt;&lt;font color=&quot;#000000&quot;&gt;August 3, 2019 From Walter DeMelle&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;font color=&quot;#000000&quot;&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;font color=&quot;#000000&quot;&gt;Notes (random) regarding possible clues to Levi Dimmell settlement in N.S.&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;font color=&quot;#000000&quot;&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;font color=&quot;#000000&quot;&gt;SOURCE:&amp;nbsp; http://www.theshipslist.com/ships/Arrivals/novascotia.shtml&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;font color=&quot;#000000&quot;&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;font color=&quot;#000000&quot;&gt;appendix: Immigration to and Emigration from Nova Scotia 1815-1838 prepared by J.S. Martell, 1942&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;font color=&quot;#000000&quot;&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;font color=&quot;#000000&quot;&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;font color=&quot;#000000&quot;&gt;Disbanded Soldiers &amp;amp; Pensioners&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;font color=&quot;#000000&quot;&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;font color=&quot;#000000&quot;&gt;J.S. Martell, &quot;Military Settlements in Nova Scotia after the War of 1812,&quot; The Nova Scotia Historical Society, Collections, Vol. 24&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;font color=&quot;#000000&quot;&gt;The Royal Newfoundland Fencibles were disbanded at Halifax in June 1816. Two captains, five lieutenants, one ensign, one quarter-master, seven sergeants, and eighty-three of the rank and file, 99 in all, accepted the offer of free land in Nova Scotia. Most of them went to the new military settlement laid out for them at Sherwood and Sherbrooke (now New Ross) in Lunenburg County.&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;font color=&quot;#000000&quot;&gt;The Nova Scotia Fencibles were disbanded at Halifax in July, 1816. Led by Captain William Ross, 172 of them are said to have arrived at Sherbrooke on August 7, 1816&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;font color=&quot;#000000&quot;&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;font color=&quot;#000000&quot;&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;font color=&quot;#000000&quot;&gt;J.S. Martell, op. cit. p.93 ff&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;font color=&quot;#000000&quot;&gt;More disbanded soldiers were placed along the projected Halifax-Annapolis Road in 1817. In April of the year, Surveyor-General Morris wrote to Captain Ross of Sherbrooke: &quot;You may soon expect —Many Setlers [sic] Germans and Highlanders on your Road between Gold River and Annapolis . . . &quot; In the same month Dalhousie asked Lord Bathurst for permission (received) to grant free land to about 50 Germans of the 7th Batt., 60th Regiment. Another military settlement on this road, Dalhousie, seems to have been started late in 1817.&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;font color=&quot;#000000&quot;&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;font color=&quot;#000000&quot;&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;font color=&quot;#000000&quot;&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;font color=&quot;#000000&quot;&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;font color=&quot;#000000&quot;&gt;28 May 1814&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;font color=&quot;#000000&quot;&gt;60th Foot Reg. 7th Batt. King&#39;s German Legion&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;font color=&quot;#000000&quot;&gt;Departure: drafted into 2nd and 3rd Battalions&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;font color=&quot;#000000&quot;&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;font color=&quot;#000000&quot;&gt;Formation of the 7th battalion&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;font color=&quot;#000000&quot;&gt;https://www.jstor.org/stable/44224645?read-now=1&amp;amp;seq=8#page_scan_tab_contents&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;font color=&quot;#000000&quot;&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;font color=&quot;#000000&quot;&gt;25 Aug 1814&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;font color=&quot;#000000&quot;&gt;Departure for Castine&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;font color=&quot;#000000&quot;&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;font color=&quot;#000000&quot;&gt;http://www.fortwiki.com/Fort_Sherbrooke_(2)&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;font color=&quot;#000000&quot;&gt;mentions Fort Sherbrooke near Castine, ME in 1814.&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/div&gt;" vertex="1">
                    <mxGeometry height="830" width="480" x="7000" y="-990" as="geometry"/>
                </mxCell>
                <mxCell id="P1l38svnj8LMgD6Zg53g-8" parent="1" style="shape=document;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;boundedLbl=1;size=0.11904761904761904;rounded=0;" value="&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: transparent;&quot;&gt;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: transparent;&quot;&gt;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: transparent; color: rgb(63, 63, 63);&quot;&gt;&amp;nbsp; &amp;nbsp; The Release of Prisoners of War from Britain in 1813 and 1814&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: transparent; color: rgb(63, 63, 63);&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;https://www.cairn.info/revue-napoleonica-la-revue-2014-3-page-118.htm#&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: transparent; color: rgb(63, 63, 63);&quot;&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;white-space: pre; white-space: normal;&quot;&gt;&lt;font color=&quot;#000000&quot;&gt;&#x9;&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;font color=&quot;#000000&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;white-space: normal;&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;white-space:pre&quot;&gt;&#x9;&lt;/span&gt;Here are some excerpts I found interesting...&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;white-space: pre; white-space: normal;&quot;&gt;&lt;font color=&quot;#000000&quot;&gt;&#x9;&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;font color=&quot;#000000&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;white-space: normal;&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;white-space:pre&quot;&gt;&#x9;&lt;/span&gt;The decisive defeat of Napoleon at the Battle of Leipzig in 1813 convinced many of his German allies to throw in their lot &lt;span style=&quot;white-space:pre&quot;&gt;&#x9;&lt;/span&gt;with the Coalition forces.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;font color=&quot;#000000&quot;&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;font color=&quot;#000000&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;white-space: normal;&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;white-space:pre&quot;&gt;&#x9;&lt;/span&gt;With the large numbers of Germans and Italians in the prison depots who were being conscripted into the forces arrayed against Napoleon, this was seen as potentially a serious problem.&lt;span style=&quot;white-space:pre&quot;&gt;&#x9;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;font color=&quot;#000000&quot;&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;font color=&quot;#000000&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;white-space: normal;&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;white-space:pre&quot;&gt;&#x9;&lt;/span&gt;There were already such nationalities being held at Valleyfield, so by August 1812 there were at least 641 Italians and 588 Germans collected at the depot.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;font color=&quot;#000000&quot;&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;font color=&quot;#000000&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;white-space: normal;&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;white-space:pre&quot;&gt;&#x9;&lt;/span&gt;The only choice these men were given was that of which force they could join. From Valleyfield they enlisted into the King’s German Legion, the 1st Dutch Regiment and most of the Italians into the army of the King of Sardinia.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: transparent; color: rgb(63, 63, 63);&quot;&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: transparent; color: rgb(63, 63, 63);&quot;&gt;An alphabetical list of American prisoners compiled by the University of Virginia is available at The National Archives and supplements a general register of American prisoners (1813) in ADM 6/417. Please note, ADM 6/417 is available on Findmypast (£).&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(63, 63, 63); background-color: transparent; white-space: pre;&quot;&gt;&#x9;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;" vertex="1">
                    <mxGeometry height="360" width="760" x="4590" y="-950" as="geometry"/>
                </mxCell>
                <UserObject label=" " link="primary_sources/WO_12-7088/P1070654.JPG" id="6DlGRqbgMNTZKLphcfNs-3">
